      "sourceText": "# Source-grounded explanation\n\n## Development input\n\nOrigin: adapted event summaries from a public discussion\nSource: https://github.com/withastro/roadmap/discussions/655\nCutoff: 2023-08-15T21:36:04Z\n\n- 2023-07-27T09:59:43Z · robak86 · opening · D_kwDOGa48bc4AVE2J: The opener reports that an Astro SSR production install carries build tools, making a minimal Node deployment much larger than expected; they ask for build-only packages to be excluded.\n- 2023-07-27T10:19:52Z · Princesseuh · comment · DC_kwDOGa48bc4AZsL7: A respondent explains that Astro's package needs those tools for CLI commands, even if a built app does not need them at runtime.\n- 2023-07-27T10:43:52Z · robak86 · comment · DC_kwDOGa48bc4AZsL8: The opener clarifies a two-stage Docker workflow: build with all tools, then install only runtime packages alongside the server output.\n- 2023-07-27T10:46:55Z · Princesseuh · comment · DC_kwDOGa48bc4AZsL9: The respondent points out that moving a library's own runtime dependencies to devDependencies would leave those packages unavailable to its users.\n- 2023-07-27T11:00:00Z · robak86 · comment · DC_kwDOGa48bc4AZsL-: The opener accepts the packaging constraint and suggests a larger separation, such as a distinct CLI package.\n- 2023-07-27T11:02:16Z · Princesseuh · comment · DC_kwDOGa48bc4AZsL_: The respondent keeps the problem open and suggests extracting a small runtime package that an SSR build could depend on alone.\n- 2023-08-15T21:36:04Z · natemoo-re · comment · DC_kwDOGa48bc4AZsMA: A maintainer moves the topic to the roadmap, supports the goal, and says the packaging design still needs discussion.\n",
